{
  "id": "L-004",
  "title": "Harder Limits: Conjugates and Common Denominators",
  "atom_ids": ["A-04"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Factoring handles the "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "0/0" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " cases where the numerator and denominator share a polynomial factor. Two other algebraic moves cover most of what remains: multiplying by a conjugate to clear a radical, and combining a compound fraction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "text": "Rationalizing with a conjugate" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "title": "The conjugate move",
      "problem": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute "
        },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "\\lim_{x \\to 4} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x} - 2}{x - 4}"
        },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Substitution gives "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{\\sqrt{4} - 2}{0} = \\dfrac{0}{0}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ", indeterminate." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\sqrt{x} + 2" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". The numerator becomes "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "(\\sqrt{x})^2 - 2^2 = x - 4" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", so the fraction is "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{x - 4}{(x - 4)(\\sqrt{x} + 2)}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The factor "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x - 4" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is only zero at "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x = 4" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", which the limit ignores, so it cancels: "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x} + 2}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Substitute "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x = 4" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ": " },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4} + 2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "widget",
      "name": "plot",
      "version": "1.0",
      "parameters": {
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "f(x) = (sqrt(x) - 2)/(x - 4)",
            "points": [
              [2.0, 0.2929], [2.5, 0.2793], [3.0, 0.2679], [3.5, 0.2583],
              [3.9, 0.2516], [4.1, 0.2485], [4.5, 0.2426], [5.0, 0.2361],
              [6.0, 0.2247], [7.0, 0.2153]
            ]
          }
        ],
        "xlabel": "x",
        "ylabel": "y",
        "title": "The hole at x = 4, limit 1/4"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "text": "Combining a compound fraction" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "title": "A compound fraction",
      "problem": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute "
        },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x + 2} - \\frac{1}{2}}{x}"
        },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Substitution gives "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}}{0} = \\dfrac{0}{0}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ", indeterminate." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Combine the numerator over the common denominator "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "2(x + 2)" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ": " },
          {
            "type": "math",
            "source": "\\dfrac{1}{x + 2} - \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{2 - (x + 2)}{2(x + 2)} = \\dfrac{-x}{2(x + 2)}"
          },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The quotient becomes "
          },
          {
            "type": "math",
            "source": "\\dfrac{-x}{2(x + 2)} \\div x = \\dfrac{-1}{2(x + 2)}"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " after cancelling the factor "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Substitute "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x = 0" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ": " },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{-1}{2(0 + 2)} = -\\dfrac{1}{4}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warning",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every cancellation in this lesson is legal only because a limit never evaluates at the point: throughout the approach, "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x \\neq a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". You are cancelling the factor — "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x - 4" },
        { "type": "text", "text": " in the first example, " },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " in the second — and the fact that it is zero at the limit point is exactly why it can be cancelled."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "formative",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute "
        },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{1 + x} - 1}{x}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " using a conjugate. Give the value and name each algebraic move you made."
        }
      ],
      "kind": "free_response"
    }
  ]
}
